---
import '../../styles/kit.css';
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import Badge from '../../components/Badge.astro';
import Button from '../../components/Button.astro';
import { href, GITHUB_URL, pageMeta } from '../../lib/site';
import { docsArticle, docsBreadcrumbs } from '../../lib/jsonld';
const h1 = "How we know it works";
---

<Page path="/docs/how-we-know-it-works" ogType="article" jsonLd={[docsArticle(h1, pageMeta('/docs/how-we-know-it-works').description, '/docs/how-we-know-it-works'), docsBreadcrumbs(h1, '/docs/how-we-know-it-works')]}>
	<main id="main" tabindex="-1">
	    <div class="bd first"><div class="wrap"><h1 class="t60">How we know it works</h1></div></div>
	    <div class="bd"><section class="wrap prose" aria-labelledby="e0" style="gap:var(--space-4)"><div class="sh"><span class="num ico" aria-hidden="true"></span><h2 id="e0" class="t28">The short answer</h2></div>
<p>We taught and tested a11ign in steps: pages we built ourselves, then a test set it had never seen, then real websites. <span class="hl">Every number here comes from real NVDA runs.</span></p>
	    </section></div>
	    <div class="bd"><section class="wrap" aria-label="The numbers"><div class="stats"><div class="stat"><div class="nm"><span>4,668</span></div><p class="lb">page captures, each read by real NVDA</p></div><div class="stat"><div class="nm"><span>1,250</span></div><p class="lb">page patterns, each built twice</p></div><div class="stat"><div class="nm"><span>231</span></div><p class="lb">pages held back for testing</p></div><div class="stat"><div class="nm"><span>129</span></div><p class="lb">real websites measured</p></div></div></section></div>
	    <div class="bd"><section class="wrap prose" aria-labelledby="e1" style="gap:var(--space-4)"><div class="sh"><span class="num ico" aria-hidden="true"></span><h2 id="e1" class="t28">Learning from pairs of pages</h2></div>
<p>We built about 1,250 page patterns. Each one exists in two versions: one accessible and one deliberately broken (2,335 and 2,333 captures). Real NVDA read every version on our own Windows machines, and we recorded exactly what it said.</p><div class="tpw"><div class="tp"><div class="th"><span>Accessible version</span><span>What NVDA said</span></div><div class="ln"><b>1.</b><span>heading, level 1, Project update</span></div><div class="ln"><b>2.</b><span>The garden project added a shaded seating area.</span></div><div class="ln ok"><b>3.</b><span>graphic, A shaded seating area beside the community garden</span></div></div><div class="tp"><div class="th"><span>Broken version</span><span>What NVDA said</span></div><div class="ln"><b>1.</b><span>heading, level 1, Project update</span></div><div class="ln"><b>2.</b><span>The garden project added a shaded seating area.</span></div><div class="ln bad"><b>3.</b><span>graphic, To get missing image descriptions, open the context menu.</span></div></div></div><p>Because every page comes as a matching pair, a11ign learns what changes in what NVDA says when one thing is broken. We also keep about 2,800 older and rejected captures, to learn from what went wrong.</p>
	    </section></div>
	    <div class="bd"><section class="wrap prose" aria-labelledby="e2" style="gap:var(--space-4)"><div class="sh"><span class="num ico" aria-hidden="true"></span><h2 id="e2" class="t28">A test set it never saw</h2></div>
<p>We held back 231 pages and captured each one twice, about 990 captures in all. They were never used for training. They exist only to check the result.</p><p>Pages we build ourselves only go so far, so the next step is real websites.</p>
	    </section></div>
	    <div class="bd"><section class="wrap prose" aria-labelledby="e3" style="gap:var(--space-4)"><div class="sh"><span class="num ico" aria-hidden="true"></span><h2 id="e3" class="t28">Real websites</h2></div>
<p>Real pages are the measurement that matters most. We have 129 so far.</p><ul><li>Across <strong>1,405 records</strong> of pages that meet the standard, the exact rules raised no false alarms. That’s our own test set.</li><li>On <strong>40 real pages</strong> that meet the standard (measured 24 September 2026), no rule failed a page wrongly, and 395 findings were passed to a person to judge.</li></ul>
	    </section></div>
	    <div class="bd"><section class="wrap prose" aria-labelledby="e4" style="gap:var(--space-4)"><div class="sh"><span class="num ico" aria-hidden="true"></span><h2 id="e4" class="t28">The quick check</h2></div>
<div class="duo"><div><div class="bignum"><span>99.71%</span></div><p class="bigcap">agreement with real NVDA on the 10 most common kinds of announcement (3,497 of 3,507).</p></div><div><p>The quick check predicts what NVDA would say, without a screen reader. We set the pass mark at 98% in advance, then tested it on a new set of pages it wasn’t tuned to, and we named every miss.</p><p>These are measurements on our own test sets, as of 17 October 2026.</p></div></div>
	    </section></div>
	    <div class="bd"><section class="wrap prose" aria-labelledby="e5" style="gap:var(--space-4)"><div class="sh"><span class="num ico" aria-hidden="true"></span><h2 id="e5" class="t28">How we test the code</h2></div>
<table class="cookt"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">What it covers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>About 1,900 unit tests</td><td>The exact rules, the judge, the report and the evaluation code. They run on every change.</td></tr><tr><td>34 labelled pages</td><td>W3C tutorial pages and paired good and bad examples, used to check the judge.</td></tr><tr><td>A false-alarm check</td><td>The exact rules must raise no false alarm on a page that meets the standard. One miss fails the check.</td></tr><tr><td>A real NVDA capture check</td><td>Real NVDA, on a GitHub-hosted Windows machine, to check the capture part still works.</td></tr></tbody></table>
	    </section></div>
	    <div class="bd alt"><section class="wrap prose" aria-labelledby="e6" style="gap:var(--space-4)"><div class="sh"><span class="num ico" aria-hidden="true"></span><h2 id="e6" class="t28">What’s next</h2></div>
<p class="lead" style="font-size:20px">a11ign is live and working today. Here’s what we’re building next.</p><ul class="grid"><li><div class="rm"><Badge>Coming soon</Badge><h3>A screen reader’s view on every commit</h3><p>A quick check that runs inside the Playwright tests you already have. No Windows machine and no extra pipeline.</p></div></li><li><div class="rm"><Badge>Planned</Badge><h3>More screen readers</h3><p>Test with the ones your users actually use. JAWS and VoiceOver are next, then Orca.</p></div></li><li><div class="rm"><Badge>Planned</Badge><h3>Whole journeys, not just pages</h3><p>Sign up, search, check out. Playwright drives the page while the screen reader listens to every step.</p></div></li><li><div class="rm"><Badge>Planned</Badge><h3>Catch the wrong voice</h3><p>Hear when a page is read out in the wrong language, or when a button’s announced name doesn’t match what’s on screen.</p></div></li></ul><p class="lead" style="font-size:20px">Star the project to follow along as each one lands.</p><div class="ctaRow"><Button size="lg" icon="github" href={GITHUB_URL}>Star on GitHub</Button><Button size="lg" variant="secondary" href={href('/get-started')}>Add to your workflow</Button></div>
	    </section></div>
	    <div class="bd"><section class="wrap prose" aria-labelledby="e7" style="gap:var(--space-4)"><div class="sh"><span class="num ico" aria-hidden="true"></span><h2 id="e7" class="t28">Read more</h2></div>
<p><a class="lnk2 solo" href="https://github.com/a11ign/a11ign/blob/main/docs/METHODOLOGY.md">The full methodology and its audit, on GitHub</a></p>
	    </section></div>
	  </main>
</Page>
